\section{Complexity and compute footprint}
We report asymptotic costs in terms of: number of micro-actions $B$, tree branching factor $b$, depth $d$, rollouts per expansion $K$, and token budget $T$.

\paragraph{IGD.} Each scheduling step computes indices across $m$ threads with amortized posterior updates. If each index evaluation costs $O(1)$ and extension cost is $O(1)$ per micro-action, total is $O(B \log m)$ with a heap; token cost scales as $O(T)$ with $T\propto B$ plus verifier calls when applicable.

\paragraph{RS-MCTT.} Selection over actions at state $s$ is $O(|\mathcal{A}(s)|)$; per-iteration rollouts cost $O(K d)$; with $E$ expansions the total is $O(E \bar{b} + E K d)$ where $\bar{b}$ is average branching per selection. With bounded $K$ and $d \le B$, the worst case is $O(B^2)$ compute steps but typically subquadratic given pruning.

\paragraph{ADR/CSC/DRSM/BB-LLM.} ADR with information-gain scoring has $O(B)$ scoring and $O(\log B)$ selection if maintained in a priority queue; CSC message passing over $q$ components with degree $\Delta$ is $O(q \Delta)$ per sweep. DRSM dual updates are $O(p)$ for $p$ subproblems per iteration with linear-time price updates. BB-LLM complexity depends on admissible bound tightness; in the best case it prunes exponentially many nodes, while in the worst case it degenerates to full enumeration; our stop rules impose a budget ceiling.

\paragraph{Memory footprint.} DEMI stores $O(M)$ records with ephemeral indices; retrieval via locality-sensitive hashing yields $O(\log M)$ expected probe cost; memory budget is enforced by eviction policies keyed to EVC contribution.

